  "query_preview": "..\nThe dry front moved through the area early this morning,\nbringing breezy to locally windy conditions with recorded wind\ngusts 30-45 mph across Southeastern Arizona. The wind is slowly\nbeginning to decrease and will linger into the early evening.\nThere is just enough mid-level moisture to allowing for a few\ncloud build ups to form currently. High resolutions CAMs like \nthe HRRR, RAP and NAM are all showing some storms to form late \nthis afternoon and evening. With how dry the low-levels are, \nvery little precipitation will make it to the ground. These \nstorms can bring some outflow winds at 30-35 mph that can pick \nup dust as well as the threat of lightning. The threat for these\nstorms will decrease after 10 pm this evening. The moisture \nsticks around tomorrow late morning and afternoon, bringing a \nchance for showers in the White Mountains. A few lightning \nstrikes are also possible with these showers with rainfall \naccumulations less than 0.25 inches.\n\nThe high pressure that is to the east will continue to build,\nkeeping temperatures 5 to 8 degrees above normal for the work\nweek. A series of shortwaves and a low pressure system passing\nto the north will come through the area bringing breezy \nafternoon winds for the second half of the work week, bringing \nnear critical fire weather condition each day.",
